{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "08", "section": "03", "chapter_title": "Course Completion and Transfer", "title": "Digital Skills Transfer Map", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section on digital skills transfer, centered on a transfer map, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the digital skills transfer map shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer", "cat100_supplement_digital_skills_transfer_map", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 3, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/08/02"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_calibration"]}, "content": "# Digital Skills Transfer Map\n\n![Digital Skills Transfer Map textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/08-03-digital-skills-transfer-map.png)\n\n<youtube-embed id=\"FF83OevoW6U\" title=\"NACE Career Readiness Competencies\" caption=\"Watch before you write the transfer row. Use the competencies to decide which skill is portable, which proof belongs with it, and which details should stay out.\"></youtube-embed>\n\n**Watch for:** Which part of your skill claim is actually portable to a future role?\n**Use this here because:** The video turns a tool name into a capability check, which is the same move this section asks you to make in the transfer map.\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThe hardest part of finishing a course is naming what you actually learned in language a future employer or professor understands. \"I learned Excel\" is nearly useless: it names a brand, not a capability, and it does not say what you can do or whether it survives outside a 40-row classroom dataset. A transfer map fixes this by converting each tool you touched into a portable capability, a proof that you did it, a real destination task where it applies, and an honest note about what changes when the context grows.\n\nThis matters because transfer is rarely a copy. The pivot table that ran cleanly over 512 class rows can break, or run dangerously slow, over 40,000 rows at a part-time job if your lookup ranges are not anchored. Knowing the click sequence is not the same as owning the skill. The transfer map is where you prove you understand a skill well enough to adapt it, which is exactly what makes it worth listing on a resume.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the digital skills transfer map shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nJordan is updating a resume after CAT 100 and writes a transfer line that reads, in full, \"Excel - my future career.\" A peer reviewer asks two questions Jordan cannot answer: \"What can you do in Excel?\" and \"Where would you use it?\" The line names a tool and a vague hope, with no capability and no destination.\n\nThe problem is not that Jordan lacks skills; it is that the line does not transfer. It cannot move into a job description because it never said what task it performs. The transfer map turns that dead line into something a reader can act on: a named capability, a file that proves it, a concrete job task, and the adaptation the new context demands.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA digital skills transfer map is a table with one row per skill and four columns that force you from a tool name toward a defensible claim of competence:\n\n| Column | Question it answers | Failure if missing |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Capability | What can you actually do, in verb form? | You list a brand (\"Excel\"), not an ability |\n| Proof artifact | Where is the file that shows you did it? | The claim is unverifiable |\n| Destination task | What real task outside class uses this? | The skill has nowhere to go |\n| Adaptation note | What changes when the context shifts? | You reveal you only memorized clicks |\n\nThe intellectual core is the difference between near transfer and far transfer. Near transfer is reusing a skill in a context almost identical to where you learned it. Far transfer is applying it where the data, scale, or stakes differ, and it requires understanding why the procedure works, not just the keystrokes. The adaptation column is where far transfer shows: a student who writes \"the formula needs an absolute reference once the data grows past a few thousand rows\" understands the mechanism; a student who writes \"use Excel\" does not.\n\nThis is also why a proof artifact is non-negotiable. A capability claim without a linked file is a wish. The map ties every claim to a real Unit file a reviewer can open, which is the same evidence standard you carry into the resume and portfolio.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A row reads: a 40-row pivot table transfers unchanged to a 40,000-row job dataset. What is wrong with it?\" explanation=\"That claim describes near transfer only and ignores what scale forces. Far transfer means naming the adaptation: a dragged lookup needs an absolute reference so the range does not drift as the data grows. An empty adaptation note reveals memorized clicks, not understanding.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Nothing; a pivot table works the same at any size.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"It skips the adaptation: a larger dataset forces an absolute reference in the lookup.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"The destination should say my future career to stay flexible.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: Building One Row of a Transfer Map\n\nA student is mapping a CAT 100 skill to a part-time retail job. The weak first draft is a single word.\n\n**Before (a tool name, not a skill).**\n\n| Skill | Transfers to |\n| --- | --- |\n| Excel | My future career |\n\nA reviewer learns nothing: no capability, no destination task, no proof.\n\n**After (capability, proof, destination, adaptation).**\n\n| Skill (capability) | Proof artifact | Destination task | What changes in the new context |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Summarize data with a pivot table | Unit 5 file pivots 512 order rows into a Region x Month grid | Build the weekly sales recap at the store | Source grows from 512 to ~4,000 rows, so lock the lookup range with an absolute reference like `=VLOOKUP(A2,$D$2:$E$4000,2,FALSE)` |\n\nThe capability replaces the brand name. The proof points to a real file. The destination names a concrete job task. The last column shows the transfer is an adaptation, not a copy: the formula that ran over 40 class rows needs an anchored range before it runs over thousands. That adaptation note is the evidence that the student understands the skill rather than just the click sequence.\n\nBuild one row of the map as a fixed procedure. Predict each step before revealing it:\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Promote a tool name into a transferable skill row\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Your draft row says only Excel. What is the first fix that turns a brand into a capability?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Add the word advanced in front of Excel\",\"Restate it as a verb phrase like summarize data with a pivot table\",\"Link your LinkedIn profile as proof\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Name the verb, not the brand. Summarize data with a pivot table is a capability; Excel is just a product.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Link a real proof artifact: the specific Unit file, like Unit 5 pivots 512 order rows into a Region by Month grid. No file, no claim.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Name a concrete destination task, such as the store\u00e2\u20ac\u2122s weekly sales recap, not my future career.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Write the adaptation note: what breaks when data grows. Lock the lookup range with an absolute reference so a dragged formula does not drift past a few thousand rows.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nNow order the four columns a complete row fills:\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"State the capability as a verb phrase, not a brand name\",\"Link the real Unit file that proves it\",\"Name a concrete destination task outside class\",\"Write the adaptation that scale or stakes force\",\"Confirm all four columns are filled honestly before listing it\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\nThese four concepts anchor the section; trace how they connect:\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer\",\"cat100_supplement_digital_skills_transfer_map\",\"evidence_based_revision\",\"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nPromote a tool to a transferable skill only when you can fill all four columns honestly. For each row:\n\n1. **Name the verb, not the brand.** \"Summarize data with a pivot table\" beats \"Excel.\" If you cannot state a verb, you have not isolated a capability.\n2. **Link a real file.** Point to the specific Unit artifact that demonstrates the capability. No file, no claim.\n3. **Stress-test the transfer.** Ask what breaks when the data is bigger, messier, or higher-stakes. If nothing changes, you are describing near transfer only; name the adaptation that far transfer would require.\n\nIf any column stays empty, the skill is not yet ready to put in front of an employer.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating the destination as \"my future career\" or \"any job.\" A destination is a specific task: the weekly sales recap, the budget reconciliation, the inventory count. Vague destinations signal that you have not pictured the skill in use.\n\nThe second misreading is assuming a skill transfers unchanged. The most revealing column is the adaptation note. A student who claims a 40-row pivot transfers untouched to a 40,000-row dataset has not understood absolute references or recalculation cost. Naming what changes is the proof of genuine, far transfer.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nBegin with one skill you genuinely used, and write the capability as a verb phrase. Resist the brand name; \"make a website\" is weak, \"publish a multi-page static site with valid HTML headings\" is a capability. The sharper the verb, the more a reader can match it to a task.\n\nNext, link the proof. Open your course files and find the exact artifact that demonstrates the capability, then reference it by unit and content, like \"Unit 5 pivots 512 order rows.\" This grounds the claim in something inspectable and keeps you honest about what you actually built.\n\nThen name a real destination and stress-test it. Pick a concrete task in a job, internship, or another course, and ask what differs there: more rows, dirtier data, a deadline, a real customer whose name appears in the data. Write the adaptation that difference forces, such as anchoring a lookup range or sampling before charting. That final note is what separates a transfer map from a tool inventory.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, draft one transfer-map row for a skill you have not seen modeled here, all the way through the adaptation column. A strong reader does not restate the pivot-table example; they can map a different skill, such as version control or HTML, and name what changes when it scales.\n\nA strong reader of the digital skills transfer map can answer four questions:\n\n1. For each skill, what is the capability stated as a verb, not a brand?\n2. Which specific file proves the capability?\n3. What concrete destination task uses it, and is the task real rather than \"my career\"?\n4. What changes about the procedure when the data or stakes grow, and why?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe map is strong when every row links a verb-form capability to a real file, a concrete destination, and a specific adaptation that demonstrates far transfer. The standard is not length; a three-row map with honest adaptation notes beats a ten-row list of tool names.\n\nA useful closing note would read: \"I mapped the pivot-table skill to the store's weekly sales recap and noted that the source grows from 512 to about 4,000 rows, which forces an absolute reference in the lookup so the formula does not drift when I drag it. I left off a row for a tool I only watched a demo of, because I have no proof artifact and cannot defend the transfer.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nApply the map itself to a non-spreadsheet skill, such as the HTML you wrote for your portfolio. The capability is \"publish a multi-page static site\"; the proof is your GitHub Pages repo; the destination might be maintaining a small business's landing page. The adaptation note now concerns something different: a real site needs a mobile-friendly layout and accessible alt text that a single class page could ignore. Reaching a correct adaptation note for a skill the worked example never covered is the test of whether you can build transfer maps independently rather than copy one.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the digital skills transfer map packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/08-03-digital-skills-transfer-map-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"digital skills transfer map\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"08.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the digital skills transfer map.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the digital skills transfer map for CAT 100 08.03. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_digital_skills_transfer_map\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your trace links claim, evidence, tool boundary, revision, and remaining limitation. That is what separates digital fluency from simple completion.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_digital_skills_transfer_map", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_digital_skills_transfer_map", "cat100_supplement_course_completion_and_transfer"], "stem": "On your transfer map, the skill column for one row just says 'Excel'. Why is this a weak transfer entry?", "options": ["It is too short to fit the table cell.", "It names a tool, not a capability; a reader cannot tell what you can actually do or where it transfers.", "Excel is outdated, so the row should say 'Google Sheets'.", "Tool names should always be in bold."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Transfer maps describe capabilities. 'Built a pivot table to summarize 500 sales rows' transfers across tools and jobs; the word 'Excel' does not.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_named_tool_is_the_skill", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which transfer-map row gives a reader a verifiable destination task?", "options": ["'HTML will help me in my future career.'", "'I can update the campus club's GitHub Pages site by editing the <h2> section headings and the nav list in index.html.'", "'Websites are useful everywhere.'", "'I learned HTML this semester.'"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A real transfer claim names the destination and the exact task. Editing specific tags in index.html is something a reviewer can check.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_transfer_without_proof", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "In class you wrote =VLOOKUP(A2,Products,2,FALSE) over 40 rows. At a part-time job the lookup table has 4,000 rows and you will copy the formula down a full column. What must you check when transferring it?", "options": ["Nothing; the formula works the same at any size.", "Lock the table range with absolute references like Products being a fixed range, or the lookup area will shift as you copy the formula down.", "Switch to a bar chart instead.", "Delete the FALSE argument because large tables are always sorted."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Transfer adapts to the new constraint. Across 4,000 rows you must anchor the lookup range (absolute reference) so copying does not slide it off the data.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_assume_identical_context", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_digital_skills_transfer_map_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "Which transfer-map cell best documents both the capability and its proof?", "options": ["'Good with spreadsheets.'", "'Capability: summarize data with pivot tables. Proof: Unit 5 file pivots 512 order rows into a Region x Month table. Destination: weekly sales recap at my retail job.'", "'Spreadsheets transfer to jobs.'", "'Pivot tables.'"], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A strong cell names the capability, points to a concrete proof artifact, and states the destination task, so a reviewer can verify the transfer.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_8_3_named_tool_is_the_skill", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "630e1472d5768643f5a71a95928ab3c0baa1464e1ca580d6441067b2263eb041", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, 